sp.p:
So has a duck-pond. He was a bit of still life; a chip; weak water-gruel;
                            a tame rabbit, boil'd to rags, without sauce or salt. He received men's
                            arguments with his mouth open, like a poor's-box gaping for halfpence;
                            and, good or bad, he swallow'd them all, without any resistance. We
                            cou'dn't disagree, and so we parted.

No, feyther's head man be gone to dextricate the cattle: but you was
                            telling I, t'other day, you do know, about a springing up of a mine;
                            which be done by a man, they do call a pye on an ear.

To the seasons it is. An English gentlewoman, of the year eighteen
                            hundred, emulates an English oak; which is hardly as well as elegant;
                            and beautiful, but bare, in the depth of December.
